WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.209
Film Courage: What suggestions do you have
for screenwriters on developing a character?

00:00:03.209 --> 00:00:07.930
Evan Kidd, Filmmaker/Podcaster: I think you
need to know what your character needs to

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.270
achieve in your broader story.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:14.879
You also need to think Okay, what would happen
if I spent time with this character?

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:16.939
I always think of it that way.

00:00:16.939 --> 00:00:21.380
Anytime I am writing a character, developing
a character I think if me and this character

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.430
went out to get a drink at a bar or something,
what would we talk about?

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:27.010
How would the conversation flow?

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.010
Would he be doing the talking?

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.120
Would I be doing the talking?

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.070
Would it be balanced/not balanced?

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.640
Would they leave after five minutes because
they have something to do?

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.080
I would always try to think about the broader
world a character inhabits outside of just

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:45.820
the script because for me that influences
a lot of the character’s decisions and things

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:49.300
that they’ll make later in the script I’m
writing.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:54.190
And I always when I develop try to do like
a word cloud if you will for a character.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:59.820
So I try to think if you just met this person
for five minutes at the grocery story, you’ve

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.610
never seen them before, what would your first
couple of takeaways be?

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:05.940
I always try to list those off.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.040
Then I think Okay what would maybe their mom
say about them, someone who has known them

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.040
for ever and ever?

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.570
So I have these two different word bubbles
and it’s always like about balancing what

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:21.000
is their kind of quick takeaways from a quick
appearance versus the deep down stuff that

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.680
they don’t let out.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.330
Film Courage: That’s really cool.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.340
Is this [the word cloud] something you got
from a book that you read or you just came

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.340
up with it?

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:29.800
Evan: It might be from a book.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.500
I’ve just kind of have done it recently
in the last couple of years.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.720
I don’t think I could call it my own formula
but it seems to work for me.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:45.030
It’s kind of a fun way to role play a little
bit with character to think Okay what would

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.030
they do?

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:52.530
How would they do things in a different way
versus like this person is mad all the time

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:58.510
because…and this person is happy because…You
are able to live in the world and world build

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:01.710
a little bit further that way (at least just
internally as a writer).

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:05.410
Film Courage: That would actually make a cool
app?

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:13.810
You just put someone’s photo and in 2 minutes
it populates what someone’s mom would say

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.810
about them and then what someone would say
about them in the grocery store.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:16.810
Evan: Yeah!

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:21.650
I like the thought of a time limit on it because
then it forces you to be very rapid fire with

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:22.650
quick takeaways.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:23.690
I love that!

